            <div xml:id="d792">

                <pb facs="0828" n="726" sameAs="frus:frus1933v03/pg726"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">393.1111 M Nielsen, Niels/41: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China (Johnson) to the Acting
                        Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1933-07-21T16:00:00+08:00">July 21, 1933—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received July 21—5:30 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>613. Following is insert for penultimate paragraph of my 612. July 21, 3 p.m.</p>
                <quote rend="blockquote">
                    <p>“The Japanese Consulate General has been instructed by Tokyo that if this
                        office presses too vigorously it is to maintain position that Japanese
                        authorities are merely intermediaries and that all responsibility <pb
                            facs="0829" n="727" xml:id="pg727"/> rests with ‘Manchukuo’. Moreover,
                        there is every reason to believe that Muto’s instructions will be of most
                        general nature and that no appreciable increase of military effort in the
                        case will result therefrom.</p>
                    <p>In the absence of a change in Tokyo’s attitude the prospect of Nielsen’s
                        early release by military operations appears slight.”</p>
                </quote>
                <p>2. Chase’s last paragraph has been referred to the Danish Minister who is being
                    kept fully informed of developments. As previously reported ransom negotiations
                    are to be left to Danish Mission without official assistance of American
                    authorities other than in obtaining unofficial assistance of the Japanese and
                    local authorities to the Mission’s negotiators.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
